Undifferentiated Thoracic Discomfort
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Atypical non-cardiac chest pain | Strictly normal serial troponins and 12-lead ECGs | Absence of reproducible chest wall/costochondral tenderness

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-044
Reason for Visit: Acute nocturnal distress and anxiety related to vague chest pressure, requiring urgent evaluation to exclude an atypical acute myocardial infarction or unstable angina.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute-onset chest tightness and pressure. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out evolving acute coronary syndrome or pulmonary embolism, preventing potentially fatal ischemic complications prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: There is this strange, heavy ache sitting right in the middle of my chest, and I can't seem to shake it or take a deep breath.
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with vague, non-radiating chest tightness. The discomfort is poorly reproducible with palpation and manifests as intermittent aching not strictly tied to exertion. The patient describes a sensation of pressure rather than a sharp pain, accompanied by an atypical pleuritic sensation that entirely lacks true respiratory variation (breathing deep does not acutely stab). There is associated mild anxiety and hyperventilation. The patient denies any associated diaphoresis, nausea, or severe dyspnea at rest. The pain has been a constant dull ache for several hours.
Functional Status: Fully independent; pacing anxiously.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe somatic anxiety regarding a heart attack.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 134/84 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Patient appears anxious, frequently touching the center of their chest, but displays no classic Levine's sign (clenched fist) and is completely hemodynamically stable. An incidental chart review notes a recent ED presentation for a subjective forceful heartbeat with transient spontaneous resolution and a normal resting ECG (undifferentiated palpitations), which was fully cleared and is separate from tonight's pressure sensation.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals a completely normal cardiac auscultation with a regular rhythm and no murmurs, rubs, or gallops. Respiratory exam shows perfectly clear lung fields bilaterally with equal air entry. Chest wall palpation does not reproduce the heavy ache. Abdominal examination is soft and non-tender, ruling out referred epigastric pain.
Environmental Observations: Safe, quiet environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking no cardiac medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Undifferentiated Thoracic Discomfort - Status: Stable, functional chest pressure likely secondary to esophageal spasm or musculoskeletal tension.
Differentials:

Acute Coronary Syndrome (ACS) - Ruled out clinically due to the complete lack of ischemic features (no radiation, no diaphoresis, no dyspnea) and normal, stable hemodynamics, though requires formal ECG correlation.

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out due to pristine room-air oxygen saturations, lack of resting tachycardia, and absence of true pleuritic stabbing pain.

Aortic Dissection - Ruled out due to the absence of a tearing/ripping sensation radiating to the back and symmetric blood pressures/pulses in all extremities.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low.

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The presentation of vague, persistent chest pressure mandated an immediate after-hours evaluation due to the inherent, unignorable risk of acute coronary ischemia. Telephone triage correctly prioritized the necessity to exclude a myocardial infarction. A thorough physical and cardiopulmonary assessment confirmed normal, stable hemodynamics, perfect oxygenation, and an absolute absence of toxic or ischemic signs. By definitively ruling out acute, life-threatening cardiopulmonary structural failure, the clinician safely de-escalated the patient's severe panic cycle, avoiding an inappropriate emergency department admission and allowing for coordinated, non-urgent outpatient cardiac stratification.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Provided physiological reassurance and administered a trial of oral antacids to rule out gastroesophageal involvement. The expected investigation results include a normal electrocardiogram and negative cardiac biomarkers, which will assist in confirming the non-cardiac diagnosis during outpatient testing.
Positive Behavioral Change: To relieve muscular and esophageal tension in the chest, drink a warm cup of herbal tea while sitting upright and performing slow, deep diaphragmatic breathing; actively relaxing the smooth muscle of the esophagus and the intercostal muscles will directly alleviate the sensation of chest pressure.
Home-Based Services: None indicated tonight.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 48 hours for a formal 12-lead ECG, blood tests, and potential stress echo referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the chest ache suddenly turns into a crushing, heavy pain that radiates to the jaw, neck, or left arm, or is accompanied by sudden sweating and vomiting.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; all non-traumatic chest pain requires a formal 12-lead electrocardiogram for absolute definitive clearance.
Clinical Course: The expected natural course involves transient, self-resolving episodes heavily influenced by stress, posture, and gastric acidity, requiring targeted lifestyle adjustments.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency attention if you feel profoundly dizzy, or if you become completely unable to catch your breath while resting.

